@section 11.5 | Satellites, Black Holes and Comets

@q 13:12

@q 13:13-15

A **geostationary** satellite orbits about **35,786 km** above the equator, eastward, with a period of **24 hours**, so it stays over one spot (communication, weather: INSAT, GSAT). **Polar sun-synchronous** satellites (about 500-900 km) pass over every place at the same local time (remote sensing: IRS, Cartosat). Satellites are tracked using the **Doppler shift** of their radio signals. During re-entry, intense heat **ionises** the air around a capsule and causes a radio **blackout**.

@q 13:73

@q 13:58

In **microgravity** there is no buoyant convection, so hot gases do not rise; a candle burns as a faint, **spherical blue flame** that may go out. The source accepts that flame shape depends on gravity.

@q 13:59-61

**Subrahmanyan Chandrasekhar** showed (1930) that a star more massive than about 1.4 Suns (the **Chandrasekhar limit**) cannot end as a white dwarf and collapses further, into a neutron star or **black hole**; he won the Nobel Prize in 1983. A comet's tail always points **away from the Sun**, pushed by sunlight and the solar wind.

@section 11.6 | Rockets, Jets and Cryogenics

@src 13:62
@pyq U.P.P.C.S. (Mains) 2007; U.P.P.C.S. (Pre) 2009
Consider the following statements in respect of a jet engine and a rocket :
1. A jet engine uses the surrounding air for its oxygen supply and so is unsuitable for motion in space.
2. A rocket carries its own supply of oxygen in the gas form and fuel.
Which of the statements given above is/are correct?
(a) 1 only
(b) 2 only
(c) Both 1 and 2
(d) Neither 1 nor 2
@ans (a)
@end

A rocket carries its oxidiser, but as a **liquid or solid**, not as gas, so statement 2 is wrong.

@q 13:64-65

Both rockets and jets work on **Newton's third law** and the **conservation of linear momentum**: gases thrown backward push the vehicle forward. A rocket works even in vacuum; a jet engine needs air.

@q 13:63

@q 13:66-68

An aeroplane wing (aerofoil) is **curved on top**, so air flows **faster over it**; by **Bernoulli's principle** faster air has **lower pressure**, and the higher pressure below gives **lift**. The Reason in the question has the velocities reversed, so it is false. The **"black box"** (flight data and cockpit voice recorders) is painted bright **orange** to be found easily.

@q 13:69-71

@q 13:72

@qnote 13:72 | All four are used as rocket propellants: **liquid hydrogen** (cryogenic fuel), **liquid oxygen** (oxidiser), **hydrazine** (storable fuel, in ISRO's Vikas engines as UDMH) and **kerosene** (RP-1, in SpaceX's Falcon and ISRO's coming semi-cryogenic engine). With no correct option, the source marks it (*).

@q 13:74

@table
Engine / fuel | Fuel + oxidiser | Example
Solid | Composite (HTPB + ammonium perchlorate) | PSLV first stage, boosters
Earth-storable liquid | **UDMH / hydrazine + N_{2}O_{4}** | Vikas engine (PSLV, GSLV)
**Cryogenic** | **Liquid hydrogen (-253°C) + liquid oxygen (-183°C)** | GSLV upper stage (CE-7.5), LVM3 (CE-20), Space Shuttle
Semi-cryogenic | Refined kerosene + liquid oxygen | ISRO's SCE-200 (under development)
@end

**Cryogenics** is the science of very low temperatures (below about -150°C). India's **indigenous cryogenic stage** first flew on GSLV-D5 in January 2014.

